.TH WHICH 1 "September 2026" "mitos-utils 0.1.0" "MITOS User Commands"
.SH NAME
which \- locate a command by searching $PATH
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B which
.I command...
.SH DESCRIPTION
For each
.I command
given, print the first executable match found on
.B $PATH
(checked in order). A
.I command
that contains a
.B /
is checked directly instead of being searched for.
.P
Prints nothing for a command that isn't found (matching
.BR "command -v" ,
not every
.B which
implementation's "no X in ($PATH)" message) so output stays script-friendly by default.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Every mitos-utils command additionally accepts \fB\-\-help\fR (print a usage line and exit) and \fB\-\-version\fR. Commands that take file/path arguments also accept \fB\-\-\fR to mark the end of options, so a file literally named e.g. \fB\-oddfile\fR can still be referenced.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if every command was found, 1 if any was not, 2 on a usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mitos\-box (1),
.BR mitos\-utils\-compat (7),
.BR mitos\-utils\-integration (7)
